     That wouldn't accomplish what he's suggesting, and it
doesn't work 100% correctly in the first place.  Those gems
which can come in alternate colors will magically change their
color in bones files if the new game choses another color from
the one which left the bones.  (A fairly trivial bug which I
don't think anyone has ever bothered to report, perhaps never
even noticed til now.  Trivial in impact on game play, that is;
I don't see any reasonable way to fix it.)

     The way the program is currently structured, having an
item where multiple instances in the same game have different
colors requires having multiple base objects of the same
type.  (Also a distinct display tile for each one.)  That's
how worthless glass is done; there are nine different types
of glass instead of one type which gets a color allocated for
each one generated.  Likewise for the dragon scales and dragon
scale mail object types, although those at least have another
difference besides just color.

     Adding more items which have multiple base types just to
vary one minor attribute seems like a bad idea.  Generalizing
existing objects to eliminate the need for extra base types
seems like a better way to spend one's time and energy (says
someone with no intention of spending any time or energy on
this...).


